ion "- Substituting $-6$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6)^{3}-6(-6)^{2}+9(-6)+1 & = \\\\ -216-216-54+1 & = \\\\ -485\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $-6$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $-6$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $0$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{3}-6(0)^{2}+9(0)+1 & = \\\\ 0-0+0+1 & = \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $0$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $0$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $2$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{3}-6(2)^{2}+9(2)+1 & = \\\\ 8-24+18+1 & = \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $2$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $2$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, there are no roots among the given options, so the correct answer is \"None\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b680bd3-e1e3-5ff5-ade3-39977cbdaaf7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that are Roots of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aeb3eef-29a8-5ea6-b548-ddcb20776a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551807} {:answer/id #uuid "487830ca-ce0a-5e85-8c66-6e544da4f594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551841} {:answer/id #uuid "67785bcb-303e-5072-8f88-6d857e799dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551906} {:answer/id #uuid "78a5611f-f5c6-5764-bfa1-f8b3d4886119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551949} {:answer/id #uuid "adb4124d-f77d-5b00-b0f0-2fbbee5b7d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adb4124d-f77d-5b00-b0f0-2fbbee5b7d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552082} :answer-field/id #uuid "52ee438e-ac14-5d2d-9ba2-e23f74a8dc8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4251c35b-5737-5f81-8168-9bb04b9bfceb" :question/math-academy-id "q-3894" :question/problem "Which of the following is a root of the polynomial ${v}^{4}-4{v}^{3}+7v-28?$\n\nI. $v=-4$\n\nII. $v=4$\n\nIII. $v=2$" :question/worked-solution "- Substituting $-4$ for $v$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{4}-4(-4)^{3}+7(-4)-28 & = \\\\ 256+256-28-28 & = \\\\ 456\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $-4$ is substituted for $v.$ Therefore, $-4$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $4$ for $v$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4)^{4}-4(4)^{3}+7(4)-28 & = \\\\ 256-256+28-28 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $4$ is substituted for $v.$ Therefore, $4$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $2$ for $v$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{4}-4(2)^{3}+7(2)-28 & = \\\\ 16-32+14-28 & = \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $2$ is substituted for $v.$ Therefore, $2$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the root is $v=4,$ so the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b680bd3-e1e3-5ff5-ade3-39977cbdaaf7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that are Roots of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a9ad680-ea7c-5349-8099-ee0f7360edf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551722} {:answer/id #uuid "1f2cf0d6-8ae2-54dc-8299-3d8f8c1181f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551731} {:answer/id #uuid "3b455006-8a18-50b3-9dfd-7f67b1c755e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551809} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6327b9-2262-5b77-8b24-265e4673cdf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552076} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1cf656-c57d-5c73-a614-b3fbde117e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f2cf0d6-8ae2-54dc-8299-3d8f8c1181f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551731} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7315726-3016-58de-b132-f1d5a281014b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "492f172a-6ec8-53da-b873-64a863435b9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-3896" :question/problem "Which of the following is a root of the polynomial ${u}^{4}-26{u}^{2}+25?$\n\nI. $u=1$\n\nII. $u=5$\n\nIII. $u=3$" :question/worked-solution "- Substituting $1$ for $u$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{4}-26(1)^{2}+25 & = \\\\ 1-26(1)+25 & = \\\\ 1-26+25 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $1$ is substituted for $u.$ Therefore, $1$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $5$ for $u$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5)^{4}-26(5)^{2}+25 & = \\\\ 625-26(25)+25 & = \\\\ 625-650+25 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $5$ is substituted for $u.$ Therefore, $5$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $3$ for $u$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3)^{4}-26(3)^{2}+25 & = \\\\ 81-26(9)+25 & = \\\\ 81-234+25 & = \\\\ -128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $3$ is substituted for $u.$ Therefore, $3$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the roots are $u=1$ and $u=5,$ so the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}]}
